## Security Rules (mandatory, no exceptions)

- Never commit `.env`. Never print `.env` values. Never expose secrets.
- Never hardcode passwords, API keys, JWT secrets, Wazuh credentials, or customer tokens.
- Never return password hashes in API responses.
- Use secure password hashing (bcrypt/argon2) and JWT (or equivalent) for authentication.
- Use role-based access control on every protected endpoint.
- Handle Wazuh/API timeouts and malformed alerts safely — never crash ingestion on bad input.
- Never expose stack traces to customers; log detailed errors server-side for SOC/admin troubleshooting.
- Prefer safe, loud failure over silent failure.

## Tenant Isolation Rules (mandatory, no exceptions)

- Every tenant-owned table has `tenant_id`; every query touching it must filter by `tenant_id`.
- Customer-facing endpoints must derive `tenant_id` from the authenticated caller, never trust a client-supplied tenant identifier without cross-checking it against the caller's own tenant.
- Customer A must never see Customer B's data under any circumstance.
- SOC/admin cross-tenant endpoints must live under admin-only routes and require an admin/SOC role.
- Any new tenant-owned table must include `tenant_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(id)` plus a supporting index, matching the existing schema style.

## Do-Not-Touch Rules

Do not modify these unless the current user request explicitly names the file/action:

- `backend-api/app/main.py`
- `docker-compose.yml`
- `postgres/init/001_mssp_core_schema.sql` (never delete/recreate/replace the schema)
- `.env` (never read its values back, never edit blindly, never commit)
- Do not restart, stop, or rebuild any Docker container unless explicitly instructed.
- Do not run `git add .` or `git commit` unless explicitly instructed in that same request.
- Do not convert the product to Streamlit; do not expose Wazuh directly to customers.

(KB-010 was granted a one-time, explicit exception to add a single `JWT_SECRET` line to `docker-compose.yml` and a 2-line router-registration edit to `main.py`. KB-011 was separately granted a one-time, explicit exception to add `Depends()` auth/RBAC checks to the 7 existing `/admin/*`/`/customer/*` endpoints in `main.py` — no `docker-compose.yml` change was needed for KB-011. Neither creates a standing exception — future edits to these files still require explicit instruction.)

